A||||1||Rrazzz||Replace clutch master or slave?||||5||
Z||000000||Rrazzz||10-22-2001||06:53 PM||||Typical problem with clutch not disengaging. I've read dozens of posts about what people have tried. I'm trying to narrow down the usual suspects to the one that is causing all (or at least most) of the problem. It always was a little bad, disengaging only when pedal was mashed all the way to the floor. Now it disengages enough to keep the car from moving when in gear w/ clutch pedal all the way down, but not enough to avoid a *crunch* when moving shifter into gear. It has about 1 1/8 to 1 1/4 inch travel at the banjo at the steel clutch pedal (1988). However, only about 7/8 inch travel at the slave. Bleeding doesn't help. So, it looks like either the clutch master cylinder or the slave cylinder is probably at fault. Any ideas on how to tell which one? Or should I just assume that based on statistics it is usually the slave. TIA<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voytek||10-23-2001||01:12 PM||||It's hard to assume either one, but:<BR>1.  Check inside the car on the firewall (front) right where the rod goes into the master cylinder.  Mine was leaky there.<P>2.  When you bleed it, do you bleed the slave cylinder too?  I.E., obviously you have someone step on the clutch pedal, but do you also push in the rod on the slave?  This must be done, otherwise you probably have air in it.<P>Other than that, I'd start with the cheaper one.  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
